# The extension SQL under extension/percolate/sql/ is generated by
# extension/percolate/build-sql.py from the spec files in specs/. It is tracked
# on purpose -- .gitignore:22 gives the reason: it is the distributable
# artifact, and a clone must be able to install without a Python toolchain.
#
# Tracking it costs a reader something, though. Over 2026-09-11/12 it was 44%
# of every line added to this repo (25,012 of 56,492, across 31 commits), and
# it appears in full in every diff and every PR view, so the change somebody
# actually wrote is found by scrolling past it. Nothing is gained by reading
# the generated copy: it is a function of the specs in the same commit, and
# drift between the two is caught by `build-sql.py --check`, not by review.
#
# So: collapsed by default, both in `git diff` (-diff) and in the GitHub PR
# view (linguist-generated). The bytes are unchanged -- these files are still
# tracked, still shipped, still installable. To read one anyway:
#
#     git diff --text -- extension/percolate/sql/
#
extension/percolate/sql/*.sql  -diff  linguist-generated=true  merge=percolate-sql

# AND THE MERGE HALF. `-diff` above stops a reader scrolling past the generated
# copy; `merge=percolate-sql` stops an AUTHOR hand-resolving it. The driver is
# dev/merge-generated-sql.sh and it does the only thing a resolution here can
# ever mean: run the generator against the merged specs and take the result.
#
# Measured over one session on eight branches: three rounds of merge-regenerate-
# resolve, main having taken twelve merges in the window, every conflict
# mechanical -- and in round three a genuine conflict in surface.sql sitting
# inside eight that were not. That is the cost: an author who has learned the
# conflicts here are noise stops reading them.
#
# REGISTER IT ONCE PER CLONE -- git will not let a repository configure its own
# driver, for the same reason it will not arm its own hooks:
#
#     git config merge.percolate-sql.name   "regenerate the extension SQL"
#     git config merge.percolate-sql.driver "dev/merge-generated-sql.sh %A %O %B %P"
#
# MEASURED, on a real conflict rather than assumed. Two branches appending to
# the same place in specs/identity/declarations.sql, so the generated files
# conflict in the same region:
#
#   * no driver registered  -- 8 conflicts: 7 generated files and the genuine
#     one in the spec. That is today's behaviour, and it is what a clone
#     WITHOUT the config gets: git falls back to its default merge, exit 1, and
#     `build-sql.py --check` still decides afterwards. Nothing silent.
#   * driver registered     -- 1 conflict, the genuine one. The seven resolved
#     by regenerating, and both branches' changes are in the result
#     (`--check` exits 0 after the spec conflict is resolved by hand).
#
# THE ONE TRAP, found by unsetting the two config keys one at a time. Setting
# `merge.percolate-sql.name` WITHOUT `.driver` does not fall back -- it is
# `fatal: custom merge driver percolate-sql lacks command line` and the merge
# does not start at all. So register both keys or neither. Registering neither
# is a supported state; half of it is not, and git says so loudly rather than
# quietly, which is the right way round.
